<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Dardanian Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">350 BCE – 28 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of the Dardani in Kosovo and the upper Vardar, a persistent threat to Macedon that allied with Rome before being absorbed into Moesia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardani</a> lived in the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scupi">Scupi</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a>) from at least the 4th century BCE. A Dardanian monarchy appears by the mid-4th century, and some historians count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardylis">Bardylis</a>, the Illyrian king who fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a>, as Dardanian.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dardanian kings led a mixed Illyrian and Thracian population of farmers and herders. They raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> repeatedly and took advantage of the Celtic invasions of 279 BCE. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longarus">Longarus</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in the 230s BCE, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bato-of-dardania">Bato</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against Philip V in 200 BCE, and King Monunius minted coins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Dyrrhachium</a>. Roman writers described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardani</a> as rough but musical.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardani</a> during the 1st century BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a> brought them under control in 28 BCE. Their land became part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> and later the late Roman province of Dardania, birthplace of the emperor Justinian.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dardanian-kingdom.jpg" alt="Dardanian Kingdom" width="320" height="309"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Q. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dardanian_Kingdom_(late_3rd_century_BC).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>350 BCE – 28 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Longarus, Bato, Monunius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Illyrian and Thracian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Illyrian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dardani, Dardania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-189&amp;focus=dardanian-kingdom" aria-label="Dardanian Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5185 664 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dardanian Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5505 920h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5505 920h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Kosovo. <a href="/atlas?year=-189&amp;focus=dardanian-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

